      <tei:div corresp="urn:x-opensiddur:text:siddur:shabbat/shacharit/ashrei">
        <tei:head xml:lang="en">Ashrei</tei:head>
        <tei:note type="instruction" xml:lang="en">Rav S.R. Hirsch writes that the six concluding chapters of the Book of Psalms are the most important gift of David's Divinely-inspired hymns that Israel trea- sures so greatly. These six psalms, 145-150, are an essential component of our daily prayers. Our Sages attach much significance to Psalm 145 in particular, and stated: “Whoever recites ‘A praise by David' three times a day is assuredly worthy of a share in the world to come” (<tei:hi rend="italic">Maseches Berachos</tei:hi> 4b). When recited in prayer, this psalm is always introduced with two verses from other Psalms (84:5 and 144:15). These verses contain the word fortunate three times, an allusion to this psalm being recited three times a day. These verses are so closely identified with Psalm 145 that it is commonly referred to as <tei:hi rend="italic">Ashrei</tei:hi> (Fortunate).</tei:note>
